{"step_index":7,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T08:00:24Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-10T13:30:24+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-10T13:30:24+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/college/College%20sem%205/ecm/ECM_COMPLETE_StudyGuide.md`\nTotal Lines: 658\nTotal Bytes: 46882\nShowing lines 1 to 658\nContent truncated: showing bytes 0-46080 of 46882. To see more, call this tool again with the same line range and ContentOffset=46080.\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Energy Conservation and Management (BE05019021)\r\n2: ## Complete Study Guide for Mid-Sem (30 Marks)\r\n3: ### Exam: 10/09/2026 | Chapters 1-3 | All Tier 1, Tier 2, and Key Tier 3 Topics\r\n4: \r\n5: ---\r\n6: \r\n7: # Chapter 1: Energy Scenario (CO-1)\n8: \n9: ## TOPIC 1: ENERGY CONSERVATION ACT 2001\n10: \n11: **Q: Discuss the salient features of Energy Conservation Act 2001. (7 marks)**\n12: \n13: The Energy Conservation Act was enacted in October 2001 in India. Its primary objective is to reduce the energy intensity of the Indian economy. The government has notified a list of designated consumers in 9 sectors, BEE star labeling, and ECBC norms under this act. \n14: \n15: Key features of the Act are:\n16: 1. The Central Government can specify energy consumption standards for appliances and equipment.\n17: 2. It mandates standards and labeling for equipment and appliances.\n18: 3. Designated consumers (large energy users) must appoint an energy manager, get energy audits done, and comply with norms.\n19: 4. It established the Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) under the Ministry of Power.\n20: 5. It defines penalties for non-compliance with the rules.\n21: 6. It introduced the Energy Conservation Building Code (ECBC) for commercial buildings.\n22: 7. State Designated Agencies (SDAs) are established for implementation at the state level.\n23: 8. The Central Government can prohibit the manufacture or sale of equipment not meeting s\n<truncated 45291 bytes>\nve sectors such as thermal power plants, iron and steel, cement, aluminum, fertilizers, textile, pulp and paper, chlor alkali, railways, refineries, petrochemicals, distribution companies, and buildings.\n607: 2. Each DC is given a Specific Energy Consumption (SEC) reduction target based on their current performance.\n608: 3. DCs that reduce their SEC beyond the target earn Energy Saving Certificates (ESCerts).\n609: 4. DCs that fail to meet their target must buy ESCerts from the market or pay a penalty.\n610: 5. ESCerts are traded on two power exchanges: Indian Energy Exchange and Power Exchange India Limited.\n611: \n612: PAT Cycle I covered 478 designated consumers and achieved energy savings of 8.67 million tonnes of oil equivalent against a target of 6.686 MTOE. This exceeded the target by about 30 percent. PAT is currently in its 7th cycle, covering over 1000 designated consumers across 13 sectors.\n613: \n614: ---\n615: \n616: # DIAGRAMS NEEDED (Questions with Drawings)\n617: \n618: The following questions need hand-drawn diagrams. Search for these on Google Images or your textbook.\n619: \n620: ## 1. CDM Project Cycle Flowchart\n621: **Question:** Write a note on CDM methodology and its project cycle with neat sketch. (7 marks)\n622: \n623: **What to draw:** A vertical or circular flowchart with 7 boxes connected by arrows:\n624: Project Design (PDD) -> National Approval (DNA) -> Validation (DOE) -> Registration (CDM Executive Board) -> Monitoring (Project Developer) -> Verification (Second DOE) -> CER Issuance (CDM Executive Board)\n625: \n626: **Google search:** `CDM project cycle flowchart diagram`\n627: \n628: ## 2. Ball Float Steam Trap\n629: **Question:** Explain ball float steam trap with neat sketch. (Part of 7-mark steam trap question)\n630: \n631: **What to draw:** Cross-section showing: trap body (chamber), inlet pipe on left, outlet pipe on right-bottom, a hollow \n<truncated 672 bytes>\n\nNOTE: The output was truncated because it was too long. Use a more targeted query or a smaller range to get the information you need.","truncated_fields":["content"]}
