{"step_index":14,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T13:30:06Z","content":"**1 Burns Classification Pathophysiology and Comprehensive Management** **Definition** A burn is an acute traumatic injury to the skin subcutaneous tissues or deeper structures caused by thermal energy flame scald contact chemical agents electricity or radiation resulting in protein denaturation cellular necrosis and systemic burn shock **Classification by Depth of Injury** **1st Degree Superficial** • Anatomy Epidermis only • Clinical Appearance Erythematous dry no blisters • Sensation Hyperesthetic painful • Healing / Intervention Heals in 3 6 days no scarring **2nd Degree Superficial Partial** • Anatomy Epidermis and Papillary Dermis • Clinical Appearance Pink / red moist thin walled bullae brisk refill • Sensation Intensely painful • Healing / Intervention Heals in 7 14 days minimal scar **2nd Degree Deep Partial** • Anatomy Epidermis and Reticular Dermis • Clinical Appearance Mottled white / red waxy dry sluggish refill • Sensation Dull pinprick sensation • Healing / Intervention Heals in 3 8 weeks with hypertrophic scar needs excision and grafting **3rd Degree Full Thickness** • Anatomy Entire Epidermis and Dermis • Clinical Appearance Leathery porcelain white / charred non blanching • Sensation Insensate anesthetic • Healing / Intervention Requires surgical excision and skin grafting **4th Degree Subdermal** • Anatomy Extends into fat fascia muscle bone • Clinical Appearance Charred blackened exposed bone / muscle • Sensation Anesthetic • Healing / Intervention Debridement flap coverage or amputation **Pathophysiology of Burn Injury** • Jacksons Zones of Burn Injury • Zone of Coagulation Central Point of maximum damage irreversible protein denaturation and tissue necrosis • Zone of Stasis Middle Compromised tissue perfusion and ischemia salvageable with adequate fluid resuscitation prone to secondary necrosis if untreated • Zone of Hyperemia Outer Peripheral vasodilation due to inflammatory mediator release viable tissue that recovers spontaneously • System\n<truncated 12741 bytes>\nirst followed by the arterial inflow clamp Confirm patency using the Acland micro forceps strip test **Common Workhorse Free Flaps** **Anterolateral Thigh ALT** • Tissue Harvested Fasciocutaneous / Myocutaneous • Vascular Pedicle Descending branch of Lateral Circumflex Femoral Artery • Major Clinical Applications Head and neck reconstruction oral cavity soft tissue defects **Free Fibula Flap** • Tissue Harvested Osteocutaneous Vascularized bone • Vascular Pedicle Peroneal Artery and Venae Comitantes • Major Clinical Applications Mandibular / Maxillary reconstruction long bone defects **Radial Forearm RFFF** • Tissue Harvested Thin pliable Fasciocutaneous • Vascular Pedicle Radial Artery and Venae Comitantes / Cephalic vein • Major Clinical Applications Intraoral lining tongue floor of mouth pharynx **DIEP Flap** • Tissue Harvested Skin and Subcutaneous Fat Spares muscle • Vascular Pedicle Deep Inferior Epigastric Perforators • Major Clinical Applications Autologous post mastectomy breast reconstruction **Latissimus Dorsi LD** • Tissue Harvested Muscle / Myocutaneous • Vascular Pedicle Thoracodorsal Artery and Vein • Major Clinical Applications Large soft tissue coverage chest wall scalp defects **Postoperative Monitoring and Flap Salvage** • Clinical Parameters Checked hourly for first 24 hrs • Color and CRT Healthy pink with 1 2 sec refill vs Pale arterial thrombosis vs Violaceous / Congested with less than 1 sec rapid refill venous thrombosis • Temperature Monitored via surface probe drop greater than 2 3 degrees C indicates perfusion compromise • Pinprick Bleeding Bright red within 1 2 sec is normal absent bleeding indicates arterial occlusion dark purple rapid bleeding indicates venous outflow obstruction • Emergency Flap Salvage Immediate return to theatre within 4 6 hours of suspected compromise Re exploration invo