l/venous cannulation sites. (filecite turn10file5 L334-L359)\n  - **Complications:**\n    - Bleeding/coagulopathy.\n    - Arrhythmias and low cardiac output.\n    - Respiratory complications.\n \n<truncated 16910 bytes>\n:**\n    - Antiplatelet/statin/anti-anginal therapy when indicated after CABG.\n    - Anticoagulation reversal after CPB with protamine as described in the source.\n    - Inotropes may be required for low cardiac output.\n    - Oxygen, fluids/electrolyte correction and rhythm treatment according to clinical need.\n    - For CABG, the source describes treatment of postoperative low cardiac output through oxygenation, preload/afterload optimisation, rhythm management and improved contractility; pharmacological or mechanical support may be needed. (filecite turn12file4 L293-L313)\n  - **Physiotherapy management:**\n    - Positioning and frequent repositioning.\n    - Diaphragmatic breathing and thoracic expansion exercises.\n    - Supported cough/huff and secretion clearance when indicated.\n    - Active ROM of limbs and ankle/foot exercises.\n    - Early mobilisation: bed mobility → sitting → standing → walking.\n    - Progressive walking and cardiac rehabilitation.\n    - Monitor HR, BP, SpO2, RR, symptoms and exercise tolerance.\n    - Stop/modify activity for new chest pain, marked breathlessness, dizziness/syncope, significant arrhythmia or haemodynamic instability.\n\n- **10. QUICK EXAM REVISION**\n  - **Open-heart surgery:** direct surgery on heart/great vessels, commonly through median sternotomy.\n  - **CPB:** temporarily takes over circulation + ventilation.\n  - **CPB flow:** venous blood → reservoir → oxygenator → pump → arterial system.\n  - **Myocardial protection:** cardioplegia, cross-clamp fibrillation, total circulatory arrest.\n  - **CABG:** bypasses coronary blockage with a graft; LIMA is important for LAD.\n  - **Aneurysm:** abnormal aortic dilatation; rupture may cause haemorrhage/tamponade.\n  - **Tamponade:** pericardial pressur