{"step_index":7,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:01:05Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nBurns & Plastic Surgery — S. Das\n\nBPT Exam Notes | Easy language, moderately explained\n\nNote: These are simplified, exam-oriented notes based on standard surgical concepts covered in S. Das. They are paraphrased rather than copied verbatim.\n\n⸻\n\n1. BURNS\n\nDefinition\n\nA burn is an injury to the skin and sometimes deeper tissues caused by heat, chemicals, electricity, radiation or friction.\n\nEtiology\n\n* Thermal: flame, hot liquid, steam, hot objects.\n* Chemical: acids and alkalis.\n* Electrical: electrical current.\n* Radiation: sunlight, radiotherapy, etc.\n* Friction: skin injury due to rubbing.\n\nIndications for Hospital/Surgical Management\n\n* Deep or extensive burns.\n* Burns involving face, hands, feet, genitalia, major joints.\n* Circumferential burns.\n* Electrical or chemical burns.\n* Inhalation injury.\n* Burns requiring skin grafting.\n\nContraindications\n\nThere are no absolute contraindications to emergency burn treatment.\nDefinitive surgery may be delayed if the patient is haemodynamically unstable or medically unfit.\n\nRisk Factors\n\n* Children and elderly people.\n* Occupational exposure to fire/chemicals.\n* Smoking and unsafe cooking practices.\n* Poor electrical safety.\n* Lack of protective equipment.\n\nTypes\n\nAccording to depth:\n\n1. Superficial burn – epidermis involved; red and painful.\n2. Partial-thickness burn – epidermis and part of dermis involved; blistering and pain.\n3. Full-thickness burn – entire skin thickness destroyed; skin may be white, brown or charred and sensation may be reduced.\n4. Deep burn – extends into deeper tissues such as fat, muscle or bone.\n\nAccording to cause: thermal, chemical, electrical and radiation burns.\n\nInvestigation\n\n* Estimate percentage of total body surface area (TBSA) burned.\n* Assess burn depth.\n* Vital signs and urine output.\n* CBC, electrolytes, renal function.\n* ABG and carboxyhaemoglobin when inhalation injury is suspected.\n* ECG in electrical burns.\n* X-ray/other imaging when associated injuries are suspected.\n\nManagement\n\nImmediate managemen\n<truncated 45615 bytes>\nment\n\nContraindications\n\nDepend on the specific operation:\n\t•\tSevere uncorrectable medical illness\n\t•\tActive uncontrolled infection\n\t•\tPatient unfit for anesthesia\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tAlcohol\n\t•\tGERD\n\t•\tObesity\n\t•\tAchalasia\n\t•\tPrevious esophageal injury\n\nTypes of Surgery\n\t1.\tHeller myotomy – commonly for achalasia.\n\t2.\tPOEM – endoscopic muscle-cutting procedure for achalasia.\n\t3.\tEsophageal dilatation – for strictures.\n\t4.\tEsophagectomy – removal of part/all of the esophagus, usually for cancer.\n\t5.\tEsophageal reconstruction – restoring food passage after esophageal removal.\n\nSurgical Procedure – Heller Myotomy\n\t•\tSmall abdominal/chest incisions are made.\n\t•\tThe tight lower esophageal muscl